    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\n`kernel_warmup()` logs `JIT kernel warmup starting.` and `JIT kernel warmup finished in %.2fs.` around `JitWarmupRegistry.warmup()`, which returns before doing anything when no registered kernel yields a compile key. Every worker of a model without registered JIT kernels logs a start and a `finished in 0.00s` for work that did not happen. The finish log is now emitted only when there were compile keys to process.\n\n## Claims\n\n- No warmup log pair on workers with nothing to warm up.\n- When there is work, the tqdm bar (`JIT kernel warmup (N compile keys)`) still marks the start and the finish log is unchanged, the same pattern CUDA graph capture uses.\n- The failure log and the compile path are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nOn main (`6ad2e06c4a`) with this commit:\n\n```text\npytest -q tests/model_executor/test_jit_warmup.py\n40 passed\n```\n\n`JitWarmupRegistry(VllmConfig()).warmup()` on an empty registry returns `False`, so neither line is logged.\n\n## Details\n\n`warmup()` now returns whether it had compile keys to process, which includes runs where every key hits the cache, so the finish log means the warmup pass ran rather than that something was newly compiled. The logs came with #50174. Returning a bool from `warmup()` keeps the decision where the compile keys are expanded; checking registrations from the caller would not see a registry whose kernels yield no keys.\n\nNo open PR addresses this. The open JIT-warmup PRs register warmups for specific kernels, and #59356 changes `jit_monitor.py` only.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are mea